<PageLayout title="My Story" description="Curiosity becoming disciplined inquiry. Why I study difficult questions, build to understand, and keep learning."><section class="page-intro dot-grid"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">My story / The person behind the lab</p><h1>Curiosity is a beginning.<br /><em>Effort makes it real.</em></h1></div></section><section class="container story-grid"><div class="story-photo"><Image src={portrait} widths={[400, 640, 960]} sizes="(max-width: 767px) 90vw, 30vw" alt="Ronit speaking at an event, holding a microphone and phone." format="webp" /><p class="photo-caption">Ronit Serva · thinker, learner, builder</p></div><div class="prose story-copy"><p class="story-lead">I want to understand how things work—and turn that understanding into something useful.</p><p>I’m Ronit, a Computer Science student at Ashoka University and a participant in The Knowledge Society. My interests span AI, financial systems, blockchain and emerging technologies. The thread connecting them is a pull toward difficult, meaningful problems.</p><h2>Ambition needed a better foundation.</h2><p>I once assumed that wanting to achieve something was enough. My perspective changed when I recognized how much ambition depends on effort, deeper learning and the willingness to question myself.</p><p>That shift still shapes how I approach a problem. I want to understand the mechanisms beneath a convincing explanation, and leave room for the evidence to change my mind.</p><h2>A real problem made building concrete.</h2><p>A financial-management difficulty close to home prompted me to design a framework and build a web app. Working on it raised more questions about how financial information can become clear, usable and trustworthy.</p><p>Today, that practical curiosity sits alongside broader investigations into agent payments and the evaluation of intelligent systems.</p><h2>How I work now.</h2><p>Notice a problem. Go back to the fundamentals. Ask where the explanation breaks. Build or design something that makes the question testable. Keep the unanswered parts visible.</p><p>It’s an approach I’m growing into. This lab is a place to share that process while it is still unfolding.</p><EditorialLink href="/explore/">See what I’m thinking about</EditorialLink></div></section><section class="container section learning-section"><div><p class="eyebrow">Where I’m learning</p><h2>Good environments.<br /><em>Harder questions.</em></h2></div><div class="learning-list"><article><span class="eyebrow">Since August 2026</span><h3>Ashoka University</h3><p>BSc in Computer Science · expected 2030</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">Innovate ’26–’27</span><h3>The Knowledge Society</h3><p>Technology, science and research</p></article></div></section></PageLayout>
